nilawati and her family have lived in  this house in lawang village in northern  aceh for 12 years it used to belong to  her grandmother  but floods have destroyed most of their  possessions including their wedding  photos  they are trying to clean up what they  can  give them  when the flood starts sometimes we have  to leave at night carrying our children  with us  more than 30 000 people across northern  aceh were affected by floods in early  january  this was nilawati's home just a few  weeks ago almost completely submerged  and not for the first time  communities in north aja experience  flooding every few months  the rain has finally stopped and flood  waters have receded now as many try to  repair their damaged homes and salvage  their possessions they're also looking  for answers  the persistent floods have ruined  people's livelihoods more than half of  the rice grown here was spoiled  what can we do we can't leave this is  our home  environmental groups say deforestation  has made north aj vulnerable to flooding  as logging on higher ground means water  runs downhill and into rivers much  faster  millions of hectares have been cleared  to make way for palm oil plantations and  many trees have also been lost to  illegal logging  we still see plantations popping up  using loopholes to acquire new land  authorities in aceh say the situation  will improve when a new reservoir is  complete  we ask the government for a reservoir to  contain the water we can't stop people  from cutting trees in the forest because  that's how they own a living  people here say the government must do  more to stop logging and help save  vulnerable communities  we don't want the government to come  here during floods and give us instant  noodles and rice we need them to think  of solutions  some say they have lost hope i have  nothing this man says the water took  everything from me  exhausted and overwhelmed these people  know it's only a matter of time before  their homes are flooded once again  jessica washington al jazeera ace
 

